  "limitation": "Partial dependence averages the model's prediction over the MARGINAL distribution of the other features: to draw the curve at UTIL_MEAN = x it takes all 20,975 training clients, overwrites that one column with x and leaves every other column untouched. When the swept feature is strongly correlated with the ones held fixed, those synthetic clients are combinations the data never contain, and the curve is then partly extrapolation rather than a description of real borrowers. This dataset is exactly that case, and here is how far it goes: the six bill columns BILL_AMT1..6 have pairwise |r| between 0.80 and 0.95 (mean 0.89); PAY_1 has |r| = 0.67 with PAY_2 and 6 features above 0.5; N_MONTHS_DELAYED has |r| = 0.83 with MAX_DELAY and 7 features above 0.5; UTIL_MEAN has |r| = 0.91 with UTIL_1 and 7 features above 0.5; LIMIT_BAL has |r| = 0.38 with UTIL_MEAN and 0 features above 0.5. Worse than correlation, two of the four are DETERMINISTIC functions of columns the sweep holds fixed: UTIL_MEAN = mean(BILL_AMT1..6)/LIMIT_BAL holds in the training data to 2.22e-16, and N_MONTHS_DELAYED = #(PAY_k > 0) holds in all but 0 of 20,975 rows, so moving either one alone produces rows that are arithmetically impossible. The two-way surface makes the same point visibly: 52 of 209 cells of the PAY_1 x LIMIT_BAL grid (24.9%) contain no training client at all, and those cells are averaged into the picture with the same weight as the dense ones. Three things are done about it rather than said about it: the Friedman H statistics are weighted by the number of training clients per cell, so empty corners cannot create an interaction; the cells with no client are marked with a cross in figures/pdp_interaction.png; and the ICE curves are shown, because a PD curve is only a mean and the ICE spread (p90 of the per-client range is 0.369) shows how much that mean hides. The honest reading is that these curves describe the MODEL's response surface, which is what the report claims about, and not the conditional expectation of default given the feature, which would need accumulated local effects (ALE) instead."
